'''Districts''' ({{langx|ms|Daerah}}; ''[[Jajahan]]'' in [[Kelantan]]) are a type of subdivision below the [[States and federal territories of Malaysia|state]] level in [[Malaysia]]. An administrative district is administered by a lands and district office ({{langx|ms|pejabat daerah dan tanah}}) which is headed by district officer ({{langx|ms|pegawai daerah}}).

==Classification==
In [[Peninsular Malaysia]], a district is a subdivision of a [[States and federal territories of Malaysia|state]]. A ''[[mukim]]'' ([[Commune (administrative division)|commune]], sub-district or [[parish]]) is a subdivision of a district. The [[National Land Code (Malaysia)|National Land Code]] assigns land matters, including the delineation of districts, to the purview of [[State governments of Malaysia|state governments]].<ref>{{Cite journal |year=2020 |title=National Land Code |url=https://faolex.fao.org/docs/pdf/mal5145.pdf |journal=[[Government of Malaysia|Malaysian federal government]] |page=54 |via=[[Food and Agriculture Organization]]}}</ref> These states operate a [[Torrens title|Torrens system]], with districts administered by the respective state's land and district office, and coordinated by the land and mines office.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Zulkifli |first1=Nur Amalina |last2=Abdul Rahman |first2=Alias |last3=van Oosterom |first3=Peter |last4=Tan |first4=Liat Choon |last5=Jamil |first5=Hasan |last6=Teng |first6=Chee Hua |last7=Looi |first7=Kam Seng |last8=Chan |first8=Keat Lim |title=The importance of Malaysian Land Administration Domain Model country profile in land policy |journal=Land Use Policy |date=December 2015 |volume=49 |pages=649–659 |doi=10.1016/j.landusepol.2015.07.015 |bibcode=2015LUPol..49..649Z }}</ref>

The state of [[Perlis]] is not divided into districts due to its size, but straight to the mukim level. The three [[Federal Territories of Malaysia|Federal Territories]] are also not divided into districts; however [[Kuala Lumpur]] is divided into several mukim for land administration purposes. [[Putrajaya]] is divided into ''precincts''.

In [[East Malaysia]], a district is a subdivision of a [[Divisions of Malaysia|division]] (''bahagian'') of a state.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.statoids.com/ymy.html |title=Malaysia Districts |publisher=Statoids.com |access-date=3 November 2010}}</ref> For example, [[Tuaran District|Tuaran]] is a district within the [[West Coast Division]] of [[Sabah]]. A district is usually named after the main town or its administrative [[Capital (political)|capital]]; for example, the town of [[Sandakan]] is the capital of the Sandakan District, as well as the capital of [[Sandakan Division]].

Some larger districts are further divided into autonomous sub-districts (''daerah kecil''; literally "small district") before the mukim level. This is prevalent in [[Sarawak]] and [[Sabah]], but also seen in Peninsular Malaysia in recent years, e.g. [[Lojing]] autonomous sub-district in [[Kelantan]]. Sub-districts in [[Sarawak]] and [[Sabah]], however, are not divided into mukim.

===Relationship with parliament and local government===
In contrast to [[Local government in Malaysia|local governments]] that manage municipal administration and infrastructure development,{{sfn|Lo Vullo|Ho|Chau|Monforti-Ferrario|2022|p=6}} districts are solely utilised for land revenue.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Adnan |first1=M Z |last2=Suratman |first2=R |last3=Samsudin |first3=S |title=Key geographical features on malaysia's land tax system: a comparison study in negeri sembilan, malaysia |journal=IOP Conference Series: Earth and Environmental Science |date=March 2021 |volume=683 |issue=1 |article-number=012095 |doi=10.1088/1755-1315/683/1/012095 |bibcode=2021E&ES..683a2095A |doi-access=free }}</ref> While districts are usually congruent with [[local government area]]s, in more urbanised states, municipalities and districts either overlap or subsume one another.{{sfn|Lo Vullo|Ho|Chau|Monforti-Ferrario|2022|p=6}} In [[Selangor]], the [[Petaling District|district of Petaling]] contains three [[List of cities in Malaysia#Cities in Malaysia|cities]] {{En dash}} [[Petaling Jaya]], [[Subang Jaya]] and the state's capital [[Shah Alam]].<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Yasin |first1=Muhammad Yazrin |last2=Zain |first2=Muhammad Azmi Bin Mohd |last3=Hassan |first3=Muhammad Haniff Bin |title=Urbanization and growth of Greater Kuala Lumpur: Issues and recommendations for urban growth management |journal=Southeast Asia |date=15 December 2022 |volume=22 |issue=2 |pages=4–19 |doi=10.1108/seamj-02-2022-b1002 |doi-access=free }}</ref> Similarly in [[Johor]], [[Johor Bahru District]] consists of three cities {{En dash}} [[Iskandar Puteri]], [[Johor Bahru]] and [[Pasir Gudang]].<ref>{{cite book |doi=10.1007/978-3-319-03644-1_6 |chapter=Spatial-Based Sustainability Assessment of Urban Neighbourhoods: A Case Study of Johor Bahru City Council, Malaysia |title=Geoinformation for Informed Decisions |series=Lecture Notes in Geoinformation and Cartography |date=2014 |last1=Ariffin |first1=Azman |last2=Mukhelas |first2=Haziq Kamal |last3=Hamid Mar Iman |first3=Abd. |last4=Desa |first4=Ghazali |last5=Mohammad |first5=Izran Sarrazin |pages=85–101 |isbn=978-3-319-03643-4 }}</ref> Conversely, a local government can administer multiple districts, such as in [[Penang]], where [[George Town, Penang|George Town]] encompasses the [[North-East District (Penang)|Northeast]] and [[South-West District (Penang)|Southwest]] districts,<ref>{{Cite news |last=McIntyre |first=Ian |date=26 Jan 2024 |title=Nearly half of Penang island's non-built land under forest cover, says council |url=https://www.thevibes.com/articles/news/100808/nearly-half-of-penang-island-to-remain-under-forest-cover-says-council |access-date=22 Dec 2024 |work=The Vibes}}</ref> and [[Seberang Perai]] comprises the [[North Seberang Perai District|Northern]], [[Central Seberang Perai District|Central]] and [[South Seberang Perai District|Southern]] districts.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Goh |first=Ban Lee |date=7 Oct 2019 |title=Seberang Perai comes of age |url=https://thesun.my/opinion-news/seberang-perai-comes-of-age-JB1452487 |access-date=22 Dec 2024 |work=[[The Sun (Malaysia)|The Sun]]}}</ref>

Administrative district boundaries also provide the basis of boundaries for the [[Dewan Rakyat|parliamentary constituencies]] in the [[Malaysian Parliament]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Persempadanan |url=https://www.spr.gov.my/ms/pilihan-raya/soalan-lazim/persempadanan |website=Suruhanjaya Pilihan Raya (Election Commission of Malaysia) |access-date=20 September 2020}}</ref> However this is not always the case; in heavily populated areas e.g. the [[Klang Valley]] and [[Kinta District|Kinta Valley]] there is serious overlap between district, local government and parliamentary boundaries.

